  "answer_text": "Yes, parathyroid disease **can** cause neck pain and discomfort, along with a wide range of other symptoms. Here's a detailed breakdown:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83e\uddb4 Can Parathyroid Disease Cause Neck Pain?\n\n**Yes**, in several ways:\n\n### 1. **Neck Lumps or Masses**\nA common symptom of parathyroid disease is a lump in the neck, as parathyroid tumors can grow anywhere along the thyroid. Later stages can cause tumors that are large enough to be felt during daily activities like swallowing or talking, which can result in discomfort or pain in the neck area.\n\n### 2. **Neck and Shoulder Pain as a Presenting Symptom**\nA 45-year-old woman who presented with non-specific neck and shoulder pain was found to have mild hypercalcemia, markedly elevated parathyroid hormone levels, and an irregular parathyroid gland on imaging. This highlights that neck pain can be one of the first signs of parathyroid disease, even in its early stages.\n\n### 3. **Bone Pain (Including the Neck/Spine)**\nThe most common symptoms of hyperparathyroidism include chronic fatigue, body aches, difficulty sleeping, bone pain, memory loss, poor concentration, depression, and headaches. Osteoporosis \u2014 a complication of parathyroid disease \u2014 can lead to broken bones and fractures. If one of these fractures happens in your spine or neck, which are particularly fragile areas, you could even experience paralysis.\n\n### 4. **Parathyroid Cancer**\nSigns and symptoms of parathyroid cancer can include pain in the abdomen, side, or back that doesn't go away, pain in the bones, a broken bone, and a lump in the neck.\n\n### 5. **Parathyromatosis**\nParathyromatosis is a condition characterized by the seeding and growth of hyperfunctioning parathyroid tissue throughout the neck or mediastinum. It can lead to recurrent and refractory hypercalcemia and ongoing management difficulties, often associated with neck discomfort.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udd0d Other Common Symptoms of Parathyroid Disease\nParathyroid disease also frequently leads to osteoporosis, kidney stones, hypertension, cardiac arrhythmias, and kidney failure.\n\n---\n\n## \u2695\ufe0f Treatment\nThere is no medicine that can cure primary hyperparathyroidism. The only way to solve the problem is with an operation, in which the bad parathyroid glands are removed. After successful surgery, symptoms \u2014 including neck and bone pain \u2014 often improve significantly.\n\n---\n\nIf you are experiencing neck pain along with other symptoms like fatigue, bone pain, or kidney stones, it is important to speak with your doctor. A simple **blood test to check calcium and PTH levels** can help determine if parathyroid disease is the cause.",
